Ìcigni sabchot`a kavshiridan ashsh-shi emigrirebuli legendaruli mcerlis, sergei dovlatovis or romans - "ch`emodansa" da "uc`xoel k`als" aert`ianebs. orive romani avtobiograp`iulia da shesanishnavi iumorit`a da gonebamaxvilobit` mogvit`xrobs sabchot`a kavshiris inteligenc`iis qop`as, emigrac`iasa da shtatebshi c`xovrebis t`aviseburebebs. saxaliso da sevdiani istoriebi, imedgac`ruebis, siqvarulis, siz'ulvilis, terorisa da t`avisup`lebis gulcrp`elad moqolili ambebi, c`oc`xali, nat`eli personazhebi realisturad da sheulamazeblad asaxavs 70-80-iani clebis sabchot`a adamianebis c`xovrebas."Ó
ÐA pebble doesn't look like much--until you know where it's been
A fascinating history of the earth takes readers on a scientific adventure through time and across the globe, all by following a single pebble over millions of years. A lively text and striking illustrations provide a revelatory account of the planet's dramatic formation."ì
éTolstoy's epic masterpiece intertwines the lives of private and public individuals during the time of the Napoleonic wars and the French invasion of Russia. The fortunes of the Rostovs and the Bolkonskys, of Pierre, Natasha, and Andrei, are intimately connected with the national history that is played out in parallel with their lives. Balls and soirees alternate with councils of war and the machinations of statesmen and generals, scenes of violent battles with everyday human passions in a work whose extraordinary imaginative power has never been surpassed.
The prodigious cast of characters, seem to act and move as if connected by threads of destiny as the novel relentlessly questions ideas of free will, fate, and providence. Yet Tolstoy's portrayal of marital relations and scenes of domesticity is as truthful and poignant as the grand themes that underlie them."û
